The numbers to memorize (best ROI for back-of-envelope)

Use these as anchors:

L1 cache hit: ~1 ns

L2 cache hit: ~4 ns

Branch mispredict: ~3 ns

Main memory (DRAM) access:.if you miss cache: ~100 ns

SSD random read (4 KB): ~20 μs (think “tens of microseconds”)

Datacenter round trip (service-to-service): ~0.5 ms

SSD sequential read (1 MB): ~1 ms

HDD seek: ~10 ms

Intercontinental RTT: ~150 ms

Bandwidth reminder: 10 Gbps sends 2 KB in ~1.6 μs, and 1 MB in ~0.8 ms (serialization time).

These anchors are enough to make 90% of interview latency arguments.

What each item means (with examples)
L1 cache reference (~1 ns)

Meaning: CPU reads data that is already in the smallest, fastest cache (L1).
Example: looping over a tight array that stays hot in cache.
Interview use: “CPU work is basically free compared to network and disk if data is cache-hot.”

Branch mispredict (~3 ns)

Meaning: CPU guessed the wrong path for an if and has to flush and recover.
Example: if (x % 2 == 0) on random data inside a hot loop.
Interview use: shows why “unpredictable branching” can hurt tight compute, but it is still tiny compared to I/O.

L2 cache reference (~4 ns)

Meaning: data not in L1 but present in L2 cache.
Example: scanning a slightly larger working set that does not fit in L1.
Interview use: helps you explain “memory locality matters.”

Mutex lock/unlock (~17 ns)

Meaning: cost of taking and releasing a lock when there is no contention (best case).
Example: protecting a counter in a single-threaded hot path.
Interview use: important caveat: the real killer is contention, where wait time can jump to microseconds or milliseconds.

Main memory reference (~100 ns)

Meaning: CPU had a cache miss, so it fetches from DRAM.
Example: pointer chasing in a linked list or hash table with poor locality.
Interview use: why cache-friendly data structures matter, and why random access is slower than sequential.

Compress 1 KB with Zippy (~2 μs)

Meaning: CPU time to compress a small chunk.
Example: compressing log/event payloads before writing or sending.
Interview use: compression can be worth it to save network or storage, but it adds CPU latency.

Read 1 MB sequentially from memory (~10 μs)

Meaning: streaming read limited by memory bandwidth, not latency.
Example: copying a 1 MB buffer in-process.
Interview use: “moving bytes” has a cost, even without network or disk.

Send 2 KB over 10 Gbps (~1.6 μs)

Meaning: pure serialization time on the wire for a small payload at 10 Gbps (does not include RTT).
Example: sending a small RPC request body.
Interview use: for small RPCs, RTT dominates, not bandwidth.

SSD 4 KB random read (~20 μs)

Meaning: point read on SSD (think key-value lookup or fetching one DB page).
Example: cache miss goes to database, database needs one page read.
Interview use: random I/O is much cheaper on SSD than HDD, but still far slower than RAM.

Read 1 MB sequentially from SSD (~1 ms)

Meaning: streaming throughput from SSD.
Example: scanning a segment file or reading a blob chunk.
Interview use: explains why sequential scans can be okay, and why batching helps.

Round trip within the same datacenter (~0.5 ms)

Meaning: one request to another service and a response back, within one DC/region.
Example: API service calls auth service then returns.
Interview use: “every extra hop costs you,” and why you reduce fanout on the critical path.

Read 1 MB sequentially from disk (~5 ms) and Disk seek (~10 ms)

Meaning: HDD behavior. Seek is the head move, sequential read is streaming once positioned.
Example: random queries on HDD get killed by seeks.
Interview use: why modern systems avoid random access on HDD and prefer SSD or log-structured access.

Read 1 MB over 1 Gbps network (~10 ms)

Meaning: transfer time limited by bandwidth at 1 Gbps.
Example: pulling data over a slow link, or large payloads.
Interview use: big payloads are expensive, so keep responses small and paginate.

TCP packet round trip between continents (~150 ms)

Meaning: cross-ocean network RTT.
Example: client in India hitting a US service, or a synchronous cross-region dependency.
Interview use: this is why multi-region calls on the critical path destroy p99.

How to use this in interviews (2 quick examples)
Example 1: “Why caching matters”

If DB is on SSD, a cache miss might cost on the order of:

service-to-DB RTT within DC: ~0.5 ms

plus SSD read: ~20 μs
So you are quickly around ~0.5 ms+ even before compute.
A cache hit avoids the SSD work and often avoids the hop.

Example 2: “Why cross-region synchronous calls are bad”

One extra cross-region dependency can add ~150 ms to p99. That dwarfs everything else.

